1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,805
Curitiba, in the south of Brazil. Friday the 19th of May, 1972, six in the evening.

2
00:00:11,505 --> 00:00:18,723
City crews arrive by truck in the old centre's main street, Rua XV de Novembro, and close it to cars.

3
00:00:20,623 --> 00:00:25,609
For years, cars and buses had filled it, and people on foot were squeezed to its edges.

4
00:00:27,309 --> 00:00:32,292
One plan was to widen the streets, knocking down buildings from the turn of the century.

5
00:00:33,992 --> 00:00:39,464
The young mayor, Jaime Lerner, an architect, wanted the street given back to people on foot.

6
00:00:40,964 --> 00:00:47,459
The shopkeepers feared the opposite: without cars, their customers would stay away, and their sales would fall.

7
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:53,713
Arguing over plans on paper couldn't show anyone what the street would be like.

8
00:00:55,613 --> 00:00:58,176
So the mayor's team built the real thing, fast.

9
00:00:59,676 --> 00:01:04,882
On Saturday morning, grey and damp, the street was handed to the people, without ceremony.

10
00:01:06,582 --> 00:01:11,961
Over the weekend, crews took up the road, laid stones, and put in streetlights and kiosks.

11
00:01:13,261 --> 00:01:15,542
They planted tens of thousands of flowers.

12
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:20,494
The next weekend, drivers came to take the street back.

13
00:01:21,794 --> 00:01:25,878
They found long strips of paper on it, and dozens of children painting.

14
00:01:27,978 --> 00:01:32,250
Within days, the shopkeepers told the newspapers their sales were up a little.

15
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:35,605
Soon they were asking for more of the street.

16
00:01:37,305 --> 00:01:41,985
Other streets followed, and fifty years on, it is still a street for people on foot.

17
00:01:43,885 --> 00:01:49,850
The city didn't win the argument on paper. It put the real thing in front of the people who had to decide.

18
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:53,682
A data team can work the same way.

19
00:01:54,782 --> 00:02:00,455
Put something real in front of the person who decides, learn from what they do with it, and do it again.

20
00:02:09,355 --> 00:02:15,938
February 2027. Graham Ellis, the university's new director of Facilities, has a request.

21
00:02:17,238 --> 00:02:21,266
Could Helena Novak's team make him a dashboard of room use, by Easter?

22
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:28,805
The question behind it is bigger: whether to ask the University Council for a new teaching building.

23
00:02:30,305 --> 00:02:33,281
It would cost about A$140 million.

24
00:02:35,181 --> 00:02:41,849
Then the request waits, as every request did: on average, 41 working days from asking to first use.

25
00:02:43,749 --> 00:02:50,090
On Tuesday the 30th of March, the team takes it up the new way, as Helena's ninety-day plan set out.

26
00:02:51,790 --> 00:02:56,791
Ivy Reyes, the team's new analyst, has already drawn its whole route on one page.

27
00:02:58,091 --> 00:03:02,891
All 186 rooms are on it, the 14 named two ways included.

28
00:03:04,591 --> 00:03:10,935
So the prototype starts from real data: thirteen weeks of bookings, and head counts from the rooms' sensors.

29
00:03:12,635 --> 00:03:16,797
Governed from the first day: counts per room, per hour, never a person.

30
00:03:20,697 --> 00:03:28,215
In 2001, the Agile Manifesto put it plainly: working software over comprehensive documentation.

31
00:03:30,115 --> 00:03:35,527
The first round asks one thing: when are the rooms booked, and when are they used, hour by hour?

32
00:03:37,227 --> 00:03:42,319
On Wednesday the 7th of April, the team's agent drafts a first model, in a minute.

33
00:03:43,619 --> 00:03:45,707
It counts booked hours as used ones.

34
00:03:47,407 --> 00:03:53,727
Ivy catches it herself: she reads the agent's notes to the end, and checks the draft against the sensors.

35
00:03:55,427 --> 00:04:01,450
Room use, as Graham means it, goes into the glossary: people in the room. The model is built from it.

36
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:05,770
Then Jun reviews Ivy's change, the same day.

37
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:13,120
Fast isn't careless. The agent proposes, people check, and no one approves their own work.

38
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:20,134
On Friday the 9th of April, ten days after the start, Graham sees the prototype.

39
00:04:21,434 --> 00:04:24,576
A heat map of the week, on a dashboard in Databricks.

40
00:04:26,676 --> 00:04:31,734
Helena asks him one question, and will ask it every round: what would change your mind?

41
00:04:33,634 --> 00:04:38,960
His answer: if the rooms are full all week, he'll ask for the building. If they aren't, he won't.

42
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:49,300
The work runs in rounds of a week, each ending on a Friday, with the person who decides and the real thing.

43
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,890
Scrum calls it a sprint review. Here, it's half an hour with Graham, at one screen.

44
00:04:57,590 --> 00:05:04,557
On Wednesday the 14th of April, Graham uses it himself, with his team: eleven working days from the start.

45
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:10,677
It had waited about seven weeks before anyone began, like every request.

46
00:05:11,977 --> 00:05:15,388
Then the new way took eleven working days, against forty-one.

47
00:05:17,288 --> 00:05:21,964
As in Curitiba, the talk changes once people can use the real thing.

48
00:05:23,864 --> 00:05:25,846
And the real thing surprises everyone.

49
00:05:26,946 --> 00:05:33,421
Rooms are booked 38% of the week, but 93% from ten to two, Tuesday to Thursday.

50
00:05:35,521 --> 00:05:41,321
In one booked hour in five, nobody comes. When people do come, about half the seats are filled.

51
00:05:43,221 --> 00:05:48,978
Everyone was right about what they saw. The average hid both the crowded peak and the empty rooms.

52
00:05:50,878 --> 00:05:57,991
At the second review, his answer moves: show me it works in 2030, when Planning expects 6% more students.

53
00:06:01,691 --> 00:06:08,280
Friday the 23rd of April, the third round. The answer is plain: the rooms are short only at the peak.

54
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:15,485
Move about one peak class in six to other hours, and release the bookings nobody uses.

55
00:06:16,785 --> 00:06:20,132
That meets the forecast to 2030 without a new building.

56
00:06:22,032 --> 00:06:26,308
The decision is served, so the rounds stop. A fourth would only polish it.

57
00:06:28,208 --> 00:06:33,105
What the prototype taught stays in the product: the definition, and the reviewed model.

58
00:06:34,605 --> 00:06:38,941
And the dashboard becomes a product of its own, which Ivy owns from July.

59
00:06:40,641 --> 00:06:44,284
In June, Graham takes one page to the University Council.

60
00:06:45,584 --> 00:06:49,889
It puts the building off for two years, and asks for the timetable to change.

61
00:06:53,789 --> 00:06:58,875
In Curitiba, a street built in a weekend settled what plans on paper couldn't.

62
00:07:00,575 --> 00:07:07,000
Helena's team did it in three weeks: real data in front of the person who decides, a round at a time.

63
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:13,477
On Monday, put something real in front of the person who decides, within two weeks.

64
00:07:14,577 --> 00:07:17,192
Use real data from the start, and govern it.

65
00:07:18,292 --> 00:07:20,895
Ask, every round: what would change your mind?

66
00:07:21,995 --> 00:07:28,028
Keep the rounds short and fixed, stop when the decision is served, and keep what you learned in the product.

67
00:07:29,528 --> 00:07:33,099
Something real in front of them in two weeks; learn; repeat.
